{"step_index":56,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T01:23:15Z","input_tokens":2119,"cache_read_tokens":105174,"output_tokens":822,"thinking":"Investigating the `MonitorTimeoutEnable` and `PerfMonitor` functionality. These appear to be Qualcomm-specific performance monitoring enhancements integrated into the ART tree. The relevant code resides in `runtime/monitor.cc`, `runtime/monitor.h`, `runtime/base/mutex.cc`, and related files. The goal is to understand the complete execution flow of these performance monitors.\n\n\nThe `MonitorTimeoutEnable` setting, controlled by command-line options, introduces a timeout mechanism for mutex operations. This timeout is enabled by default for the system server and can be activated for other applications via a debugging flag. It leverages a timed `futex` wait, a key difference from standard lock profiling, allowing for stack dumps upon timeout. This implies the feature actively detects and reports potential lock contention issues that might otherwise go unnoticed with standard AOSP tools.\n\n\nThe code initiates a synchronous checkpoint request to the lock owner thread upon timeout, crucial for capturing live stack traces within the QSSI's `PerfMonitor` system and related AOSP ART mechanisms. This checkpointing allows the logging of detailed contention information including thread IDs and wait times. It will verify that timeout flags are properly propagated from Zygote, specifically checking for the presence of the `AndroidRuntime.cpp` file within the 8397 project to understand the `-X` and `-XX` flag handling.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"grep -rn \\\"MonitorTimeout\\\\|LockProf\\\\|Perfetto\\\\|ThreadSuspend\\\" /home/ethen/workspace/voyah/projects/8397/code/qssi/frameworks/base/core/jni/AndroidRuntime.cpp 2>/dev/null || true\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Check AndroidRuntime.cpp flags passed to ART\""}}]}
